{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The claim is **too broad to assess without specifying a country, year, and income measure**.\n\nFor example, in the United States, Asian households have historically had a higher median household income than households overall. But that does not establish higher average individual earnings: household income depends partly on household size and the number of earners, and a median is different from a mean.\n\n“Asian people” also covers many populations with substantial income differences. An aggregate statistic can be accurate while obscuring those differences, and it says nothing about any particular person’s income."}}
